Vulnerability Heading

Broken Access Control Leading to Unauthorized Exposure and Download of Internal Configuration Repository Files

Severity

High

CVSS v3.1

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:L/A:N

Base Score: 7.4 (High)

(If downloaded files contain sensitive configuration or credentials, severity may be increased to 8.1 High.)

Vulnerability Category

Broken Access Control / Improper Authorization

OWASP Top 10 (2025)

A01:2025 – Broken Access Control

CWE
CWE-285 – Improper Authorization
CWE-639 – Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key
CWE-200 – Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor
Description

During the security assessment, it was observed that an authenticated user with the Analyst role could access internal repository locations by directly manipulating the client-side URL and navigating to:

#explore/SysConfig

This exposed the internal FileSystemRoot and multiple configuration directories containing XML and DTD files intended for administrative use.

Although the application correctly displayed the message:

"The current user does not have the privilege to download files."

when the Analyst user attempted to download files, it was observed that the download permission was controlled by a client-side attribute. By intercepting the response and modifying the download permission value from:

data-download="false"

to

data-download="true"

the download icon became enabled and restricted files such as:

dwp.dtd
asset.dtd
user.xml
pubStories.xml

could be downloaded successfully.

This indicates that authorization checks are enforced on the client side, while the backend fails to perform proper server-side authorization validation before granting access to sensitive configuration files.

Steps to Reproduce
Authenticate as an Analyst user.
Manually browse to:
https://<host>/swing/app/swing.html#explore/SysConfig
Observe that internal configuration repositories are accessible.
Navigate to:
SysConfig → Metadata
Attempt to download any file and observe the following message:
The current user does not have the privilege to download files.
Intercept the response using Burp Suite.
Modify the client-side permission value:
data-download="false"

to:

data-download="true"
Forward the response.
Click the download icon.
Observe that restricted files are downloaded successfully.
Impact

A low-privileged authenticated user can:

Enumerate internal repository structures.
Access administrative configuration directories.
Download XML and DTD files intended for privileged users.
Obtain information regarding internal application structure and metadata.
Facilitate further attacks by leveraging disclosed information.
Potentially expose sensitive configurations if such information is present within downloadable files.

The vulnerability demonstrates insufficient server-side authorization enforcement and reliance on client-side controls to restrict access to sensitive resources.

Mitigation
Enforce authorization checks on the server side for all repository access and file download operations.
Do not rely on client-side attributes or UI controls to determine permissions.
Return HTTP 403 Forbidden when unauthorized users request restricted resources.
Restrict access to SysConfig and FileSystemRoot repositories to privileged roles only.
Implement centralized RBAC validation for repository browsing and download functionality.
Review all APIs associated with repository management to ensure consistent access control enforcement.
Log and monitor unauthorized access attempts to sensitive resources.
