Vulnerability Heading

Broken Object-Level Authorization Leading to Unauthorized Team Enumeration and Cross-Team Article Creation

Severity

High

CVSS v3.1

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N

Base Score: 8.1 (High)

Vulnerability Category

Broken Access Control / Broken Object-Level Authorization (BOLA)

OWASP Top 10 (2025)

A01:2025 – Broken Access Control

CWE
CWE-639 – Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key
CWE-285 – Improper Authorization
CWE-862 – Missing Authorization
Description

During the security assessment, it was observed that an authenticated user (Ravi) was authorized to create articles only within three assigned teams.

However, the application relied on a user-controlled parameter (krbId) while fetching the list of accessible teams:

GET /services/film/teams/teamList?krbId=poojaz&articleLangId=eng

By modifying the krbId parameter from the authenticated user to another valid user, the application returned the list of teams associated with that user without performing proper server-side authorization validation.

Subsequently, the attacker was able to select those unauthorized teams and successfully create articles belonging to teams for which they were not authorized. The created articles were marked as:

External Team – You are a collaborator

This demonstrates that authorization decisions are based on user-supplied identifiers rather than the authenticated session context, resulting in unauthorized access to business objects across team boundaries.

Steps to Reproduce
1. Login using a low-privileged Analyst account.

Only three teams are available during article creation.

Figure 1: Authorized teams available to the authenticated Analyst user.

2. Intercept the request used to retrieve team information.

Original request:

GET /services/film/teams/teamList?krbId=<authenticated_user>&articleLangId=eng

Modify:

krbId=poojaz

The response contains teams belonging to another user.

Figure 2: Manipulating the krbId parameter returns teams associated with another user.

3. Use one of the returned team names while creating a new article.

The unauthorized teams are displayed in the article creation interface.

Figure 3: Unauthorized teams become available for article creation.

4. Create an article using the unauthorized team.

The article is successfully created and visible under:

External Team – You are a collaborator

Figure 4: Successful cross-team article creation using unauthorized team membership.

Impact

Successful exploitation allows an authenticated low-privileged user to:

Enumerate teams belonging to other users.
Create articles under unauthorized teams.
Bypass team-based segregation controls.
Access or manipulate research content associated with other business units.
Violate confidentiality and integrity of research workflows.
Potentially interfere with article approval and publication processes.
Compromise tenant and organizational boundary isolation.
Mitigation
Do not rely on client-supplied identifiers (krbId) for authorization decisions.
Derive user identity exclusively from the authenticated session or Kerberos principal.
Enforce server-side authorization checks before returning team information.
Verify that the requesting user is a member of the requested team before permitting article creation.
Implement object-level access control validation on all team-related APIs.
Log and monitor cross-user resource access attempts.
Perform periodic access-control reviews to ensure proper team segregation.
