Absence of Rate Limiting Allows Message Flooding Through Automated Requests

Severity

Medium

CVSS v3.1

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:H

Base Score: 6.5 (Medium)

Vulnerability Category

Improper Rate Limiting / Resource Consumption

OWASP Top 10 (2025)
A04:2025 – Insecure Design
CWE
CWE-770 – Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling
CWE-799 – Improper Control of Interaction Frequency
CWE-400 – Uncontrolled Resource Consumption
Description

During the assessment, it was observed that the internal messaging functionality lacked sufficient rate-limiting controls. After intercepting the message sending request, the request was replayed automatically using Burp Suite Intruder.

More than 300 consecutive requests were successfully processed by the application without any restriction, CAPTCHA, throttling, account lockout, or response delays. As a result, hundreds of messages were delivered to the recipient mailbox.

The application failed to enforce controls to limit the number of messages that can be sent within a specific time period, allowing attackers to abuse the functionality for message flooding and denial-of-service against users.

Impact

Successful exploitation could allow an authenticated attacker to:

Flood user inboxes with thousands of unwanted messages.
Cause denial of service against the messaging system.
Exhaust application resources.
Disrupt normal user operations.
Conduct spam campaigns using the internal messaging platform.
Increase storage and database utilization.
Affect overall application performance.
Steps to Reproduce
Navigate to the Messages section.
Create a new message and send it to a valid recipient.
Capture the message sending request using Burp Suite.
Send the request to Burp Intruder.
Configure automated requests.
Execute more than 300 requests.
Observe that all requests return:
{
    "result":"ok"
}
Verify that hundreds of messages are successfully delivered to the recipient inbox.
Impact

An attacker can abuse the messaging functionality to:

Spam users.
Generate excessive notifications.
Consume storage resources.
Degrade service availability.
Launch application-layer denial-of-service attacks.
Mitigation
Implement server-side rate limiting for message APIs.
Restrict the number of messages allowed per user per minute/hour.
Introduce throttling mechanisms after excessive requests.
Apply CAPTCHA or additional verification for abnormal activity.
Monitor and alert on suspicious messaging behavior.
Implement anti-spam controls and quotas.
Return HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) when thresholds are exceeded.
Consider user reputation or adaptive rate limiting mechanisms.
