Use of Vulnerable and Outdated jQuery Library (Version 2.2.4)

Severity

Medium

CVSS v3.1

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N

Base Score: 6.1 (Medium)

Vulnerability Category

Vulnerable Components / Third-Party Component Risk

OWASP Top 10 (2021)

A06:2021 – Vulnerable and Outdated Components

OWASP Top 10 2025 (Draft)

A06 – Vulnerable Components

CWE
CWE-1104 – Use of Unmaintained Third-Party Components
CWE-937 – Using Components with Known Vulnerabilities
Description

During the assessment, it was observed that the application uses jQuery version 2.2.4, as disclosed within the client-side JavaScript resources.

The identified version is outdated and no longer maintained. Several security issues affecting jQuery 2.x versions have been publicly disclosed, including Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities and improper HTML parsing behavior.

Applications relying on outdated JavaScript libraries may inherit known vulnerabilities that can be leveraged by attackers depending on how the affected APIs are used within the application.

Impact

Successful exploitation may allow an attacker to:

Execute arbitrary JavaScript in users' browsers.
Perform DOM-based Cross-Site Scripting attacks.
Manipulate page content.
Steal session tokens or sensitive information.
Compromise user accounts through client-side attacks.

The actual impact depends on whether vulnerable jQuery APIs are used by the application.

Mitigation
Upgrade jQuery to the latest supported version (3.7.x or later).
Remove deprecated APIs and test application compatibility.
Maintain an inventory of third-party components and regularly update dependencies.
Implement Software Composition Analysis (SCA) to identify vulnerable libraries.
Review application code for unsafe uses of:
.html()
.append()
.prepend()
.before()
.after()
