Session Token Exposure in URL Parameters

Severity

Medium

CVSS v3.1

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:L/A:N

Base Score: 6.5 (Medium)

Vulnerability Category

Sensitive Information Exposure / Session Management Weakness

OWASP Top 10 (2021)

A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures

OWASP Top 10 2025 (Draft)

Broken Authentication

CWE
CWE-598 – Use of GET Request Method With Sensitive Query Strings
CWE-200 – Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor
CWE-384 – Session Fixation
Description

During the assessment, it was observed that the application transmits the user's session token within the URL query string. The following request contains an authentication token as a GET parameter:

GET /swing/api/rest/conf/channel/list?token=71348d8a-ca01-446c-a846-ec7130a476f2&application=swing&showCompound=true&showCalendar=true&databaseId=33 HTTP/1.1

Sensitive tokens embedded in URLs may be exposed through:

Browser history.
Web server access logs.
Reverse proxies and load balancers.
Referrer headers sent to third-party websites.
Browser bookmarks.
Monitoring and analytics tools.
Shared screenshots and clipboard operations.

An attacker obtaining the token may potentially replay requests and gain unauthorized access until the token expires or is invalidated.

Impact

Successful exploitation may allow an attacker to:

Hijack active user sessions.
Replay authenticated requests.
Access sensitive resources without credentials.
Bypass authentication mechanisms.
Leak tokens through logs, browser history, and third-party services.
Compromise confidentiality and integrity of application data.
Mitigation
Avoid transmitting session identifiers or authentication tokens in URL parameters.
Store session tokens in:
Secure, HttpOnly cookies; or
Authorization headers (Bearer tokens).
Ensure tokens are:
Short-lived.
Randomly generated.
Invalidated after logout.
Rotated periodically.
Configure applications to prevent sensitive information from being written to logs.

Implement:

Secure
HttpOnly
SameSite

cookie attributes where applicable.

